{"passages":[{"reference":"5.6","text":"And I said, “Then are the things said by poets and philosophers correct: that in Hades the souls of the impious, after being judged for what they dared to do, are punished—such as the souls of Ixion, Tantalus, Tityus, Sisyphus, the daughters of Danaus, and all the others who ever acted impiously here? And if these things are not so, how could magic have any reality?” When he said that matters in Hades were indeed so, I asked, “Why, then, have we ourselves not feared magic, since we are convinced of the punishment in Hades for adultery? For I do not agree that it is right to use magic to force a woman who is unwilling. But if someone promises to persuade her, I am quite ready for that and to acknowledge my gratitude as well.”","lexical_decisions":[{"source_quote":"δυνατὸν ἂν μαγείαν ὑφεστάναι","chosen":"how could magic have any reality","rationale":"ὑφεστάναι has an existential sense here: magic could not subsist or be real if the premises about Hades were false."},{"source_quote":"καὶ αὐτοὶ μαγείαν οὐ πεφοβήμεθα","chosen":"have we ourselves not feared magic","rationale":"The first-person plural is retained as an inclusive rhetorical question; αὐτοί adds emphasis, ‘we ourselves.’"},{"source_quote":"ἐπὶ μοιχείας πεπεισμένοι τὴν ἐν ᾅδου δίκην","chosen":"since we are convinced of the punishment in Hades for adultery","rationale":"ἐπὶ μοιχείας identifies adultery as the ground for the punishment; πεπεισμένοι governs the conviction expressed by τὴν ... δίκην."},{"source_quote":"πρὸς τοῦτο μετὰ τοῦ καὶ χάριν ὁμολογεῖν ἑτοίμως ἔχω","chosen":"I am quite ready for that and to acknowledge my gratitude as well","rationale":"ἑτοίμως ἔχω expresses readiness, while χάριν ὁμολογεῖν means to acknowledge gratitude or indebtedness."}],"source_issues":[]},{"reference":"5.7","text":"And Appion said, “Do you not think it amounts to the same thing whether you obtain her by magic or by persuading her with words?” I said, “They are not at all alike; each is far removed from the other. The man who compels an unwilling woman by magic’s force incurs the harshest punishment as one who has schemed against a chaste woman. But the man who persuades her by speech and makes his appeal depend on her own freedom and choice has used no force. Still, I think the persuader will be punished less than the man who used force. So if you can persuade her, I will be grateful if I succeed; otherwise, I would rather die than forcibly compel a woman who does not consent.”","lexical_decisions":[{"source_quote":"αὐτῆς ἐπιτύχοις","chosen":"you obtain her","rationale":"With a person as the genitive object in this erotic context, ἐπιτυγχάνω means to attain or succeed in obtaining that person."},{"source_quote":"ἐπὶ τῇ αὐτῆς ἐξουσίᾳ καὶ βουλῇ ποιησάμενος τὴν παράφασιν","chosen":"makes his appeal depend on her own freedom and choice","rationale":"παράφασις can denote an appeal, exhortation, or persuasion. The ἐπί phrase makes her own authority and will the basis on which the appeal succeeds; the defensible alternative ‘makes the persuasion subject to her own freedom and will’ preserves the same contrast with force."},{"source_quote":"ἧττον δὲ οἶμαι κολασθήσεσθαι τὸν πεπεικότα τοῦ βιασαμένου","chosen":"I think the persuader will be punished less than the man who used force","rationale":"The genitive τοῦ βιασαμένου supplies the standard of comparison after ἧττον."},{"source_quote":"χάριν ἕξω ἐπιτυχών","chosen":"I will be grateful if I succeed","rationale":"Literally, the speaker will ‘have gratitude’ upon succeeding; English expresses this naturally as being grateful."},{"source_quote":"ἥδιόν μοι τεθνάναι μᾶλλον ἢ","chosen":"I would rather die than","rationale":"The comparative wording is emphatic in Greek; idiomatic English expresses the preference with ‘rather ... than.’"}],"source_issues":[]},{"reference":"5.8","text":"And Appion, genuinely at a loss, said, “What am I to say to you? At one moment, as a man tormented by love, you wish to obtain her; at another, as if you were not in love, you put fear before desire. You imagine that after persuading her you will be free from liability as though sinless, but that if you obtain her by the force of magic, you will incur punishment. Yet you fail to understand that what is judged in every action is its outcome—the fact that it happened—not how it happened. And if you commit adultery, surely you will not be judged to have acted impiously if you manage it by magic, yet be absolved of the sin involved in adultery if you persuade her.” I said, “Because I am in love, obtaining the woman I love is in any case my settled aim. I must therefore choose one of the two, and, if possible, I will choose to flatter rather than use magic. But it is not easy to persuade her even with flattery, for the woman is thoroughly a philosopher.”","lexical_decisions":[{"source_quote":"ἀληθῶς διαπορῶν","chosen":"genuinely at a loss","rationale":"διαπορέω conveys serious perplexity or uncertainty about how to proceed."},{"source_quote":"τῆς ἐπιθυμίας προτιμᾷς τὸν φόβον","chosen":"you put fear before desire","rationale":"The genitive marks the thing over which fear is preferred; ‘prefer fear to desire’ is an equivalent alternative."},{"source_quote":"ὡς ἀναμάρτητος ἔσεσθαι ἀνεύθυνος","chosen":"you will be free from liability as though sinless","rationale":"ἀνεύθυνος means not liable or not accountable, while ὡς ἀναμάρτητος presents the supposed basis for that exemption."},{"source_quote":"τὸ τέλος παντὸς πράγματος κρίνεται ὅτι γέγονεν","chosen":"what is judged in every action is its outcome—the fact that it happened","rationale":"τέλος denotes the resulting end or outcome. The following ὅτι γέγονεν clarifies that the occurrence itself, rather than its manner, is in view."},{"source_quote":"μαγείᾳ μὲν δυνηθεὶς","chosen":"if you manage it by magic","rationale":"The participle δυνηθείς leaves the action implicit; the context supplies succeeding in the adultery through magic."},{"source_quote":"πάνυ γὰρ ἐστὶν ἡ γυνὴ φιλόσοφος","chosen":"the woman is thoroughly a philosopher","rationale":"πάνυ intensifies the predicate φιλόσοφος; ‘very much a philosopher’ is more literal but less idiomatic."}],"source_issues":[]},{"reference":"5.9","text":"And Appion said, “For that very reason I am more confident that I will be able to persuade her as you yourself wish, if indeed we can speak with her.” I answered, “Impossible.” Appion asked whether it was possible to write to her. I said, “That is possible.” Then Appion said, “Before this night is over, I will compose a book, an encomium to adultery. You will take it from me and send it on to her, and I hope that, once persuaded, she will consent.” Appion composed that book and gave it to me. It was about this book that I thought during this night, and at just the right time I was reminded that I had it among the other books I carry with me. After relating these things, I showed the book to those present and read it aloud when they wished to hear it. And then, after reading it, I said, “Gentlemen, this is the education of the Greeks, with a noble theme for sinning without fear.” The book ran as follows:","lexical_decisions":[{"source_quote":"Ταύτῃ μᾶλλον εὔελπίς εἰμι","chosen":"For that very reason I am more confident","rationale":"Ταύτῃ is taken causally as referring to the woman’s being a philosopher. It could more formally be rendered ‘in this respect,’ but the causal connection is contextually stronger."},{"source_quote":"Ἔτι τῇ νυκτὶ ταύτῃ","chosen":"Before this night is over","rationale":"ἔτι with the temporal dative indicates that the composition will occur while the present night still lasts; ‘this very night’ is an equivalent alternative."},{"source_quote":"πεισθεῖσα συνθήσεται","chosen":"once persuaded, she will consent","rationale":"συντίθεμαι here means to agree or consent to the proposed relationship."},{"source_quote":"καὶ εὐκαίρως ἔχειν αὐτὸ ὑπεμνήσθην","chosen":"at just the right time I was reminded that I had it","rationale":"εὐκαίρως is taken with the act of being reminded. A possible alternative attachment is ‘I was reminded that I conveniently had it,’ but the temporal reading is more natural in context."},{"source_quote":"σὺν ἄλλοις βιβλίοις οἷς μεθ’ ἑαυτοῦ ἐπιφέρομαι","chosen":"among the other books I carry with me","rationale":"The relative οἷς is understood by case attraction to βιβλίοις, and the reflexive phrase is interpreted contextually with the first-person verb."},{"source_quote":"ἡ τῶν Ἑλλήνων παιδεία","chosen":"the education of the Greeks","rationale":"παιδεία may also mean Greek culture or intellectual formation; ‘education’ preserves the immediate connection with the philosophical book just read."},{"source_quote":"γενναίαν ὑπόθεσιν ἔχουσα πρὸς τὸ ἀδεῶς ἐξαμαρτάνειν","chosen":"with a noble theme for sinning without fear","rationale":"The wording is ironic: γενναίαν calls the book’s ὑπόθεσις ‘noble,’ while the purpose or tendency expressed by πρός is fearless wrongdoing."}],"source_issues":[{"source_quote":"οἷς μεθ’ ἑαυτοῦ ἐπιφέρομαι","issue":"The confirmed printed wording has the third-person reflexive ἑαυτοῦ with the first-person verb ἐπιφέρομαι. Later Greek can extend ἑαυτοῦ beyond strict third-person reference, so the supplied wording remains intelligible as ‘with me’; the classically expected first-person reflexive would be ἐμαυτοῦ. The proposed reading is therefore only a conjectural normalization, not a correction to the selected edition.","proposed_reading":"οἷς μετ’ ἐμαυτοῦ ἐπιφέρομαι","blocks_publication":false}]},{"reference":"5.10","text":"The Lover to His Beloved
(Anonymously, because of the laws originating from foolish people)

At the commands of Love—the oldest of all—for the sake of a child. I have learned that you devote yourself to philosophy and, for virtue’s sake, emulate the life of those who are superior. And who among all beings could be superior to the gods, and who among human beings could be superior to the philosophers? For philosophers alone know which actions are evil or good by nature, and which, though they are not so, are regarded as such through the enactment of laws. Take, for example, the act called adultery: some have supposed it to be evil, although it is good in every respect, for it takes place at Love’s commands for the multiplication of life. Love is the oldest of all gods. For without Love no mingling or generation can take place—of elements, of gods, of human beings, of irrational animals, or of all the remaining things. Indeed, we are all instruments of Love. But the craftsman himself, working through us and present in the souls of every being that is generated, is Mind. Consequently, it is not of our own volition that we desire to do his will, but when we are commanded by him. And if, while desiring by his will, we attempt for the sake of so-called self-control to restrain our desire, how will we not commit the greatest acts of impiety by opposing the oldest of all gods and human beings?","lexical_decisions":[{"source_quote":"Ὁ ἐρῶν τῇ ἐρωμένῃ","chosen":"The Lover to His Beloved","rationale":"The nominative sender and dative recipient form an epistolary heading. ἐρωμένη denotes the woman loved or desired."},{"source_quote":"ἀνωνύμως διὰ τοὺς ἐκ τῶν ἀνοήτων ἀνθρώπων νόμους","chosen":"Anonymously, because of the laws originating from foolish people","rationale":"ἐκ marks the foolish people as the source of the laws; ‘because of the laws made by foolish people’ is an equivalent contextual rendering."},{"source_quote":"ἐπιταγαῖς Ἔρωτος τοῦ πάντων πρεσβυτάτου παιδὸς χάριν","chosen":"At the commands of Love—the oldest of all—for the sake of a child","rationale":"The main rendering takes Ἔρωτος with ἐπιταγαῖς, treats τοῦ πάντων πρεσβυτάτου as describing Love, and construes παιδὸς χάριν as the separate phrase ‘for the sake of a child.’ Because the compressed heading permits competing attachment, τοῦ πάντων πρεσβυτάτου παιδός could alternatively be understood together as ‘the oldest child of all,’ though that leaves the function of χάριν less clear."},{"source_quote":"τίνες δὲ ἂν εἶεν κρείττονες ἢ θεοὶ μὲν ἁπάντων, ἀνθρώπων δὲ οἱ φιλόσοφοι;","chosen":"And who among all beings could be superior to the gods, and who among human beings could be superior to the philosophers?","rationale":"The rendering takes ἤ after comparative κρείττονες as ‘than’ and understands ἁπάντων and ἀνθρώπων partitively, expanding the elliptical second half to preserve the parallel rhetorical comparison. A viable alternative takes ἤ as ‘except’ or ‘other than’: ‘And who could be superior except the gods among all and the philosophers among human beings?’ Another possible construal treats the genitives comparatively, making the gods superior to all and philosophers superior to other humans."},{"source_quote":"οὗτοι γὰρ μόνοι ἴσασιν","chosen":"For philosophers alone know","rationale":"The demonstrative most naturally resumes the immediately preceding philosophers, who possess the claimed philosophical discernment. It could grammatically encompass both gods and philosophers, but the argument is directed to the recipient’s philosophical learning."},{"source_quote":"ποῖα δὲ τῇ τῶν νόμων θέσει (οὐκ ὄντα) νομίζεται","chosen":"which, though they are not so, are regarded as such through the enactment of laws","rationale":"The omitted predicate is supplied from ‘evil or good’; the parenthetical οὐκ ὄντα denies that the actions possess by nature the qualities assigned by law."},{"source_quote":"εἰς γὰρ τὴν τοῦ βίου πολυγονίαν","chosen":"for the multiplication of life","rationale":"πολυγονία denotes abundant generation or production of offspring. ‘For life’s abundant procreation’ is a more literal alternative."},{"source_quote":"οὐ ζῴων ἀλόγων","chosen":"of irrational animals","rationale":"ἄλογος contrasts nonhuman animals as lacking reason; ‘non-rational living creatures’ is an equivalent alternative."},{"source_quote":"παντὸς τοῦ γεννωμένου ψυχαῖς ἐπιδημῶν","chosen":"present in the souls of every being that is generated","rationale":"ἐπιδημέω literally denotes residing, sojourning, or being present among; here it describes Mind’s presence in the souls of generated beings."},{"source_quote":"ἐστὶ νοῦς","chosen":"is Mind","rationale":"νοῦς is capitalized because the passage identifies it as the craftsman operating through all beings; ‘intellect’ or lowercase ‘mind’ would preserve the lexical sense but less clearly convey the personified cosmic role."},{"source_quote":"ὑπ’ αὐτοῦ κελευσθῶμεν, τὸ ἐκείνου βούλημα","chosen":"we are commanded by him ... his will","rationale":"The pronouns immediately follow the identification of the craftsman as Mind, while the argument also closely identifies that craftsman with Love’s activity. The English preserves the masculine referent without forcing a sharper distinction between Love and Mind than the passage supplies."},{"source_quote":"χάριν τῆς λεγομένης σωφροσύνης κρατεῖν τῆς ἐπιθυμίας","chosen":"for the sake of so-called self-control to restrain our desire","rationale":"σωφροσύνη can mean self-control, moderation, or chastity. ‘Self-control’ suits the immediately following attempt to master sexual desire, while ‘so-called chastity’ remains a viable context-specific alternative."}],"source_issues":[]}]}
